DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       This program is part of NNeettppbbmm(1).

       ppnnmmttoottiiffffccmmyykkreads  a  PNM  image  as input and produces a CMYK encoded
       TIFF file as output.  It optionally  modifies  the  color  balance  and
       black level, and modifies removal of CMY from under K.

       Output  is  to  Standard  Output, but unlike with most Netpbm programs,
       Standard Output must be a seekable file.  An ordinary file is fine, but
       you  cannot  pipe the output to another program.  Furthermore, the pro‐
       gram replaces any content currently in the file even if it  was  opened
       for appending.

       ppaammttoottiiffff generates many other kinds of TIFF files.



OOPPTTIIOONNSS
       In  addition  to  the options common to all programs based on libnetpbm
       (most notably --qquuiieett, see
        Common Options ⟨index.html#commonoptions⟩ ), ppnnmmttoottiiffffccmmyykk  recognizes
       the following command line options:

       The  order of most options is not important, but options for particular
       conversion algorithms must appear after the algorithm is selected (--ddee‐‐
       ffaauulltt,--nneeggaattiivvee).   If you don't select an algorithm, ppnnmmttoottiiffffccmmyykk as‐
       sumes --ddeeffaauulltt and the appropriate options (--tthheettaa,--ggaammmmaa,--ggaammmmaapp)  can
       appear anywhere.


   --nnoonnee,,--ppaacckkbbiittss,,--llzzww,,--pprreeddiiccttoorr
       Tiff  files  can be compressed.  By default, ppnnmmttoottiiffffccmmyykk uses LZW de‐
       compression, but (apparently) some readers cannot read this, so you may
       want  to  select a different algorithm (--nnoonnee,--ppaacckkbbiittss).  For LZW com‐
       pression, a --pprreeddiiccttoorr value of 2  forces  horizontal  differencing  of
       scanlines before encoding; a value of 1 forces no differencing.


   --mmssbb22llssbb,,--llssbb22mmssbb
       These options control fill order (default is --mmssbb22llssbb).


   --rroowwssppeerrssttrriipp
       This  sets the number of rows in an image strip (data in the Tiff files
       generated by this program is stored in strips  -  each  strip  is  com‐
       pressed  individually).  The default gives a strip size of no more than
       8 kb.


   --lloowwddoottrraannggee,,--hhiigghhddoottrraannggee
       These options set tag values that may be useful for printers.


   --kknnoorrmmaall,,--kkrreemmoovvee,,--kkoonnllyy
       These options control the calculation of the CMYK ink levels.  They are
       useful only for testing and debugging the code.

       --kkrreemmoovvee  sets the black (K) levels to zero while leaving the other ink
       levels as they would be if the black level were normal.

       --kkoonnllyy sets all inks to the normal black value.


   --ddeeffaauulltt,,--nneeggaattiivvee
       These options control what ink levels ppnnmmttoottiiffffccmmyykk uses  to  represent
       each input color.

       --nneeggaattiivvee  selects  a simple algorithm that generates a color negative.
       None of the following options apply to this algorithm.   The  algorithm
       is  included  as  an example in the source code to help implementors of
       other conversions.

       --ddeeffaauulltt is not necessary, unless you have to countermand  a  --nneeggaattiivvee
       on the same command line.

       The default conversion from RGB to CMYK is as follows: The basic values
       of the 3 pigments are C = 1-R, M = 1-G, Y =  1-B.   From  this,  ppnnmmttoo‐‐
       ttiiffffccmmyykk chooses a black (K) level which is the minimum of those three.
       It then replaces that much of the 3 pigments with the black.   I.e.  it
       subtracts K from each of the basic C, M, and Y values.

       The options below modify this conversion.


   --tthheettaa _d_e_g
       --tthheettaa  provides  a simple correction for any color bias that may occur
       in the printed image because, in practice, inks do not exactly  comple‐
       ment  the primary colors.  It rotates the colors (before black replace‐
       ment) by _d_e_g degrees in the color wheel.  Unless you are trying to pro‐
       duce unusual effects you will need to use small values.  Try generating
       three images at -10, 0 (the default) and 10 degrees and see  which  has
       the best color balance.


   --ggaammmmaa _n
       --ggaammmmaa  applies  a  gamma  correction  to the black (K) value described
       above.  Specifically, instead of calculating the K value as min(C,M,Y),
       ppnnmmttoottiiffffccmmyykk raises that value (normalised to the range 0 to 1) to the
       _nth power.  In practice, this means that a value greater than  1  makes
       the  image lighter and a value less than 1 makes the image darker.  The
       range of allowed values is 0.1 to 10.


   --ggaammmmaapp _n
       This option controls the black replacement.

       If you specify --ggaammmmaapp, ppnnmmttoottiiffffccmmyykk uses the specified gamma value in
       computing  how  much  ink to remove from the 3 pigments, but still uses
       the regular gamma value (--ggaammmmaa option) to generate the  actual  amount
       of black ink with which to replace it.

       Values of _n from 0.01 to 10 are valid.

       For  example,  it  may  be best to only subtract black from the colored
       inks in the very darkest regions.  In that case, _n should  be  a  large
       value, such as 5.

       As  a  special case, if _n is -1, ppnnmmttoottiiffffccmmyykk does not remove any pig‐
       ment (but still adds the black ink).  This means dark  areas  are  even
       darker.   Furthermore,  when  printed,  dark areas contain a lot of ink
       which can make high contrast areas, like lettering, appear fuzzy.  It's
       hard to see what the utility of this is.


